{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1474964-apex-alternate-setting-campaign","id":1474964,"title":"APEX - Alternate Setting & Campaign","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-02-19T05:36:19-08:00","id":1474964,"title":"APEX - Alternate Setting & Campaign","content":"This is the campaign I'm running for my family. I am pretty sure none of them are on these forums but if the name <i>&quot;Detective Dumbshot&quot;</i> or quote <i>&quot;Pizza, quesadilla, mother####er&quot;</i> means anything to you - stop reading as there are spoilers ahead.<br /> <br /> OK, so I'm a fan of grim, conspiracy-ridden, nineties/noughties era super-hero comics, campaigns, &amp; settings. I loved the original Aberrant with it's shades-of-grey, imperfect/greater-good Utopia, imperfect heroes, imperfect world take on the genre. So whilst I'm happy with (most of) the new rules, the campaign I'm running with my boys has a different setting with different (though rules-compatible) reasons for the setting's super powers.<br /> <br /> I'm running a group of Talents with one (low-level) Nova. In my setting, Talents are not <i>&quot;Flux powered probability wizards&quot;</i>. Inspiration, Gifts, etc are like Momentum - purely meta/game explanations for the cool things that action heroes pull off. Nova powers, on the other hand, <b><i>do</i></b> share a singular origin. Which I'll go into later as the campaign starts revealing bits of it. For now...<br /> <br /> <b>---</b><br /> <br /> <i>The APEX setting is set in 2030 (i.e. eight years from now) and in the intervening time we've recovered from the current plague... and suffered from a more virulent, more deadly one. Vaccinations, when they came about, saved us from a much worse result but the population was decimated. On the back of that, a small (0.1%?) number of those who recovered did so with crippling &amp; highly visible mutations. These mutations have a deleterious effect on the person's life expectancy BUT come with random &quot;meta abilities&quot; (i.e. the &quot;afflicted&quot; are low-level novas, 75pts instead of 150pts in chargen). These &quot;Afflicted&quot; don't have long to live (some dying in mere months), but whilst they live they can be anything from super-strong... to being capable of melting steel with a mere glance.<br /> <br /> Which of course, as gritty worlds are wont to do, leads to all hell breaking loose. Scientists want to understand how the powers work, govt's want to round up Afflicted for reasons of public safety (&amp; national security), religions are battling with what these powers mean in regards to their view of the universe, and criminal/war-driven organisations kidnapping &amp; selling them as commodities. Enter Apex Biochem.<br /> <br /> Apex Biochem was a small, relatively unknown pharmaceuticals research company with a handful of patents and a minor hand in helping develop the vaccine to the disease that wiped out a tenth of the world's population. Two months ago, they reveal to the world seven individuals with <b>massively</b> (i.e. full 150pt chargen) increased meta-abilities whose only abnormal appearance is their bright blue glowing eyes. Apex Biochem states that they are the successful result of a process they've developed to cure Afflicted whilst maintaining their ability to wield (&amp; improve on) the powers that caused their health problems. Called &quot;Reborn&quot; by Apex Biochem (a name quickly adopted by the media) - these seven people prove to be quite powerful, healthy, and cured of their previous afflictions. And with Apex' lawyers &amp; marketing ensuring the courts of judicial process &amp; public opinion are onside - the govt is, currently, unable to justify just kidnapping these &quot;Reborn&quot; or acquiring the process that created them through force.<br /> <br /> My kids are playing a small, deniable team of assets brought together by someone claiming backing from the govt. They have money &amp; resources, they know the characters, and they are willing to help them achieve their personal end-goals so long as they work to discredit Apex Biochem &amp;/or find out how they've accomplished what they're doing. Think &quot;The Boys&quot;, but with a bit more X-Files conspiracy and less 'diabolical' uber-violence (yet).</i><br /> <br /> <b>---</b><br /> <br /> That's it for now. I'll be going into the characters next time (tomorrow or the next day), their backgrounds, and Session Zero (introducing them to the Storypath Talent/Aberrant system). In the meantime, have the character sheets I put together for the game. Slightly re-arranged because the boys wanted some things on the front page of the character sheets for easier location during gameplay. We also use cards for their powers/edges, for which I created some templates I can put up next time. Having cards with a summary of the edge/gift/specialty/power details is also a great timesaver during play.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://docdro.id/9s2BHUI\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Talent Sheet</b></a><br /> <a href=\"https://docdro.id/Eih07C9\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Aberrant Sheet</b></a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-20T23:48:45-08:00","id":1475085,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\"><i><b>Player Characters:</b></i></span><br /> <br /> So this is a family game. I have a &quot;boys night&quot; every Friday with my sons where we either play video/board/rpg games together or watch bad action/horror movies. Keeps me in touch as they start to spread their wings (two are in university, one near the end of high school). Session Zero was about putting together characters that would be fun to play, have an impact in the world (without giving too much away), and some basic tutorial on the system we'd be playing.<br /> <br /> I helped the boys put together their characters and their inexperience with the system really highlighted how much I wish the books had been arranged better. I like the system, but yeah, the boys pointed out it could use some work for people who don't have the time to read the books through three times like I have.<br /> <br /> In the end, we came up with the following characters -<br /> <br /> <b>The Cleric</b><br /> My eldest is playing a Talent from a deeply conservative set of the Catholic Church. Think Christian Bale from Equilibrium - right down to the gun-kata style build. His sect is one trying to push a religious rebellion against the Afflicted &amp; Reborn and his character is (at the start of the campaign at least) a devout believer. The interesting thing here is that my son wants the character's views to change - his long term aspiration is to form a positive relationship/bond with one of the setting's supers. He's the most experienced TTRPG player in the group.<br /> <br /> <b>The Wick</b><br /> The other talent is a modified John Wick, complete with a Continental-style secret organisation of mercenaries. Combat, contacts, and style are his thing. Hell, his current short term aspiration is to find himself a new dog (yup, he hates the Afflicted because one killed his dog). This player has run a Chronicles of Darkness campaign for his friends, so knows the basics of Storyteller/Storypath which helped.<br /> <br /> <b>The Shifter</b><br /> My youngest is playing a low-level nova (75pts chargen), a journalist from South America who's eruption event is a bit shrouded in mystery. Their &quot;abnormality&quot; is a body of non-fixed form, slightly shifting whenever they're not concentrating to maintain a single form. Their powers are all based around their body's less than solid nature - Morph, Boost, Plasticity, and Homunculus. He's trying to find out how/why he erupted given he has no memory of the event (unlike other setting Afflicted who all recall it being a massively painful incident). He is splitting his character goals between finding out what happened to him and bringing down corruption with a Watergate style expose.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><i><b>Session One:</b></i></span><br /> <br /> Only one of my boys has experience with a d10 dice-pool system (Chronicles), so I thought I'd take it easy on them and have the first session be a bit of a play-test, help them work out how the game works, how their characters work, and generally get a feel for action-oriented Storypath. As such, it was a little rail-roady, but everyone was onboard for that so that everyone had a feel for the system.<br /> <br /> The characters are called for a meet through their contacts (note passed during Mass for the Cleric, a go-between for the Wick, and an address+time at a tip-drop for the Shifter). The Wick scopes out the meet (a warehouse on the docks), whereas the other two just show up on time at the front door. The Wick wants to show off and asks for an alternate entrance he can use to get in a back way, the first Dramatic Edit of the campaign.<br /> <br /> In the warehouse they meet their contact. Nicol\u00e1s Furia. Totally not a Mexican Mr Jackson rip-off so I can lay the one-liners on thick &amp; fast. After basically introducing himself as the contact for their employer (stated to be the govt), he provides a reason for them each to join - a letter from Catholic Cardinal for Cleric, standard contract &amp; promise of Afflicted targets for Wick, and a combination of blackmail &amp; inside info for Shifter. After it's established the team will work together, the wall of the warehouse explodes in and a team of heavily armed soldiers in tactical armour start firing on the lot of them.<br /> <br /> More Dramatic Editing has the two combat Talents equipped and conveniently in cover because they always would be. Furia steps back behind some shelves. We go over a basic firefight, covering standard action mechanics, cover, range, and using Gifts &amp; Inspiration to deal with being outnumbered two to one by skilled professionals. Shifter works out how to use their powers, how to max out to go 'above &amp; beyond', and how even with Boosted Dexterity + 3 dice of Momentum it is still possible to completely fumble catching a tossed pistol. <br /> <br /> After the battle, they go to interrogate one of the soldiers only to watch his head turn to red mist. Cleric spots multiple snipers at positions covering all the exits and spends an Inspiration to have the SUV Furia arrived in be bullet-proof. They drive away form the scene using it's newly solid-steel canopy as protection.<br /> <br /> <i>End Session One</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-21T10:59:15-08:00","id":1475124,"title":"","content":"I love this!!! This is so cool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-21T23:51:30-08:00","id":1475198,"title":"","content":"<i><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Session Two:</b></span></i><br /> <br /> A few days have passed in game and Not-Nick-Fury (NNF) is keeping his distance to prevent being tracked to the players who are deniable assets. A text on their burner phone has the team pick up some details from a dead-drop. Apparently, a local went crazy, <i>picked up a car</i> and threw it into a cafe before promptly having a heart-attack &amp; keeling over in the street before the police arrived.<br /> <br /> NNF's message says that an FBI team will be picking up the body from the medical examiner in a few hours and intends to cover-up APEX Biochem involvement (though none has been suggested, yet). To prevent the govt from being able to completely destroy the details, the team needs to get the body, any blood samples, and the medical examiners preliminary reports.<br /> <br /> The team decides covert is (obviously) the best way to handle this. Some Dramatic Editing has the details from NNF's package include extensive details on the two detectives police in charge of the case. 'Detective Dumbshot' (a jaded detective driven to divorce &amp; alcohol by the job) and 'Detective Mendosa' (a young firebrand who was partnered with Dumbshot for going up against the wrong senior officers in her early years).<br /> <br /> Shifter uses the details on Dumbshot to Morph into a copy of the man and heads to the morgue. Wick gets in contact with a 'cleaning service' in his underground contacts in order to get a fake id &amp; cleaning truck to bring to the morgue (for taking the body). Cleric heads to Dumbshot's apartment to intercept the real detective before he can go to work. I flick between each of these scenes a few actions at a time to keep the ball rolling in realtime. I manage OK but I realise that splitting the team into two is probably the most I can handle and keep everyone properly engaged.<br /> <br /> Shifter gets to the morgue (looking like the detective), only to find Mendosa waiting there for him. Bluffing his way into her accepting him as the real deal is relatively easy through judicious use of non-verbal communication and the fact he looks EXACTLY like the target (given 6 successes on the Morph roll + Enhancements) - the two head inside to see the ME, who tells them that the dead perp shows none of the usual signs of Affliction on the table, is a known gangbanger (so definitely not one of APEX's media-friendly Reborn), and died of what looks like massive heart failure at the age of 26yrs. The blood tests are yet to leave the building so no details on them, but they're due to be picked up in the hour by the courier. This was run as a minor Intrigue sequence with the auto-success being the cause of death &amp; non-Afflicted nature of the body, the rest coming from a &quot;1 success = 1 answer&quot; style of getting more details.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Wick needs to do a favour for the fake ID on such short notice. The contact says he'll do it in exchange for the team loading a trojan from USB into the morgue computer systems. Being able to intercept and alter the digital lodgement of death certificates can come in very handy for a papers man and his previous inside contact who did it manually is &quot;indisposed&quot; (read: caught &amp; arrested). Wick is going to have his cleaning services truck &amp; ID to get into the morgue, but he'll have to make a detour and load the code onto one of the morgue PC's before leaving!<br /> <br /> Finally, Cleric has made a cakewalk out of his task delaying Detective Dumbshot. Intercepting him at the apartment door, he injects him with a sleeping drug mixed in with a short-term memory inhibitor (Well-Equipped is an awesome Edge). Dragging him to bed and splashing a little alcohol on his pillow, the idea is he wakes up in the afternoon groggy, smelling of liquor, and with no/little memory of the last 24 hours. Of course, a complication arises in the form of Detective Dumbshot's estranged daughter showing up at his door and Cleric spinning a story of bringing the detective home from a bar the night before, and just arriving now to check up on him. Angry that her father has started drinking again, she goes to leave (to live on the street) but Cleric is a priest first &amp; foremost, so takes her to a local hostel run by the parish. Little does he know this girl is going to become a major complication in his life!<br /> <br /> Back at the morgue, Wick has gotten into the building, spent Inspiration an abandoned office with PC's still logged in, and loaded the trojan onto their network from the USB. As an Intrigue sequence, he decides he wants to hack more details of the perp &amp; the autopsy from the system. I give him the free success (I want him to get the filed autopsy report), but he rolls only one other success (he specced the character for combat, not computer hacking!). That one extra success lets him know the blood samples were being redirected to another pathology lab than the usual... a subsidiary of APEX Biochem.<br /> <br /> Parking his cleaning cart outside the autopsy room (large enough to store a body hidden underneath), Wick creates a distraction by starting an argument with Mendosa (using Inspiration to Dramatically Edit a sensitivity to sexist comments). Taking a loud dressing down (&amp; a Complication on police noticing him for a week or two moving forward), Wick attracts all attention to himself allowing the Shifter to get the body of the dead car thrower into the cart AND to stretch their arm through a hole in the reception window to grab the blood vials meant for pathology. After which they make they leave with no-one (yet) the wiser.<br /> <br /> <i>End Session Two</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-25T05:26:36-08:00","id":1475447,"title":"","content":"I was supposed to run a session tonight, but had some work to get done, so playing tomorrow afternoon. In the meantime, I thought I'd share the template document we use for creating cards for describing powers, gifts, etc. We've found that having a handful of your special abilities sleeved with poker or Magic cards really helps during gameplay. Instead of trying to squish everything into the character sheet or flip through the rule book all the time, just scan through your handful of cards for the relevant details. Some cards have multiple abilities/edges on them as the description is short enough to do so.<br /> <br /> It's a LibreOffice Draw file, so easy to grab the software for editing it &amp; convert to PDF as needed. When I print them out, I actually rotate the cards 90\u00b0 as I can lay out six to a page then (saving printer paper). Just grab a copy of the card that best suits what you're trying to write up and type away.<br /> <br /> <b>Cards Template</b><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://easyupload.io/ovcu4k\" target=\"_blank\"><b>ODG File Link</b></a><br /> <a href=\"https://docdro.id/hAFnfp8\" target=\"_blank\"><b>PDF Preview</b></a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}